WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.617 --> 00:00:18.618
All types of students from all over the world

00:00:18.619 --> 00:00:22.522
And often I'm talking about math and science

00:00:22.523 --> 00:00:24.957
and i say something that usually surprises people

00:00:24.958 --> 00:00:29.395
I will tell students and other people that I get goosebumps doing math

00:00:29.396 --> 00:00:34.500
I always have. Even as a kid, I loved doing math problems

00:00:34.501 --> 00:00:37.103
Well uh, people sometimes say to me, "Well how did that happen?"

00:00:37.104 --> 00:00:42.108
And I tell a story. You know we all love the products of our child experiences.

00:00:42.109 --> 00:00:47.280
Things that happened to us as kids, and I was very fortunate to have really wonderful parents.

00:00:47.281 --> 00:00:51.984
Who enjoyed reading to me when I was a very little kid

00:00:51.985 --> 00:00:56.289
And I started reading very early. And loved the reading.

00:00:56.290 --> 00:01:06.365
Well, somehow my parents decided they wanted to see how to connect the reading to playing with money and with objects involving math

00:01:06.366 --> 00:01:12.738
And before I knew it, when I was fairly fairly young, I was solving math word problems

00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:15.208
Involving money, loving it, loving it.

00:01:15.209 --> 00:01:21.314
Understanding that if I had 3 dimes and my dad had 2 quarters

00:01:21.315 --> 00:01:25.651
and we'd put all of our money together, that we'd have $0.80.

00:01:25.652 --> 00:01:29.889
And learning later on that that was the same as 80% of a dollar.

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:31.457
Or that it was 8 over 10

00:01:31.458 --> 00:01:38.598
And so all of a sudden, I began to see how language and math came together

00:01:38.599 --> 00:01:43.002
But I also began to understand how you could think about math

00:01:43.003 --> 00:01:45.104
and anything you do.

00:01:45.105 --> 00:01:47.273
If you look at the number of people in a room

00:01:47.274 --> 00:01:48.875
If you look at the weight of people

00:01:48.876 --> 00:01:53.179
If you look at the room itself, and you come to understand shapes and you see rectangles

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:56.382
and squares and you think about the area in a room, and the perimeter.

00:01:56.383 --> 00:02:01.087
All these terms that you hear, in some class. In geometry, or in algebra.

00:02:01.088 --> 00:02:06.626
And for me, what was interesting was that most students didn't like math.

00:02:06.627 --> 00:02:14.934
And in class, as a little kid, I would watch other students either being bored or frustrated

00:02:14.935 --> 00:02:17.403
because they weren't understanding the problem.

00:02:17.404 --> 00:02:20.940
And so I did something that was especially helpful to me

00:02:20.941 --> 00:02:23.442
in deciding what I wanted to do for the rest of my life.

00:02:23.443 --> 00:02:31.317
I began to tutor kids, I began to get really excited about the idea that maybe I could help somebody else

00:02:31.318 --> 00:02:36.622
to understand the problem. Or to grasp a concept.

00:02:36.623 --> 00:02:38.524
Or to get excited about the work.

00:02:38.525 --> 00:02:41.194
I wanted more people excited about math because I just thought it was fun.

00:02:41.195 --> 00:02:49.235
And what became clear to me was that it was much harder to explain something to another student

00:02:49.236 --> 00:02:53.072
than it was simply for me to understand it myself.

00:02:53.073 --> 00:02:56.542
And a-ha! Then I realized here's my challenge:

00:02:56.543 --> 00:03:02.281
I have to figure out how to use language that is so clear and simple

00:03:02.282 --> 00:03:07.220
that I can explain a concept, because I understand the concept myself well,

00:03:07.221 --> 00:03:11.891
that I can get someone else to not only understand whatever it is the teacher is talking about

00:03:11.892 --> 00:03:15.895
but then to help that student learn how to solve the problem

00:03:15.896 --> 00:03:22.335
and to develop confidence. Because what was also clear was that because I had exposure to math

00:03:22.336 --> 00:03:28.474
at home and reading. And because I was always solving math problems, I was getting better and better.

00:03:28.475 --> 00:03:32.178
The lesson there was that we learn to do by doing.

00:03:32.179 --> 00:03:35.915
I found that the more I read, the better a reader I became

00:03:35.916 --> 00:03:40.486
And the more I enjoyed reading, quite frankly, the more reading I did.

00:03:40.487 --> 00:03:42.054
The same thing in mathematics.

00:03:42.055 --> 00:03:46.893
When a student seems to feel less than confident about the math

00:03:46.894 --> 00:03:51.364
it's usually because they've gotten confused about a problem or have not had success.

00:03:51.365 --> 00:03:57.904
All of a sudden, by the time I was 13, I was in a special math program
at a university for the summer.

00:03:57.905 --> 00:04:01.340
And the professor came into the class, this was at Tuskegee,

00:04:01.341 --> 00:04:04.810
and it was a  National Science Foundation program for smart kids in math.

00:04:04.811 --> 00:04:08.581
And all of a sudden, first of all, I realized I wasn't the smartest person in the world

00:04:08.582 --> 00:04:13.886
that there were a lot of people who had done even more than I had and I was inspired by that.

00:04:13.887 --> 00:04:18.157
But the professor came in and he put a math problem on the board

00:04:18.158 --> 00:04:21.527
and we all looked at it and nobody could solve it.

00:04:21.528 --> 00:04:24.530
And he said, "See me when you can solve it." And he left.

00:04:24.531 --> 00:04:29.435
And people were really frustrated, because they were thinking,

00:04:29.436 --> 00:04:32.004
"The teacher's supposed to help you solve the problem."

00:04:32.005 --> 00:04:34.340
And his point was, "No, you work on it."

00:04:34.341 --> 00:04:39.712
And I found myself less frustrated and much more fascinated.

00:04:39.713 --> 00:04:44.083
Because what he was saying was, I had the ability if I really worked on it,

00:04:44.084 --> 00:04:48.254
to solve that problem. Even though I didn't have a clue about how to do it.

00:04:48.255 --> 00:04:51.757
So we spent days working on this problem and statistics

00:04:51.758 --> 00:04:56.696
And what I learned was that even though we couldn't get the complete answer

00:04:56.697 --> 00:04:59.899
we learned so much in struggling with the issue.

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:02.768
Well, the reason math is so fascinating to me, in general,

00:05:02.769 --> 00:05:09.775
is that it teaches you to look at a problem from different perspectives.

00:05:09.776 --> 00:05:12.712
And not necessarily to be so frustrated,

00:05:12.713 --> 00:05:16.015
because you find yourself not able to solve it

00:05:16.016 --> 00:05:19.619
I also found as I went through college, quite frankly,

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:23.923
was that math was at the foundation of all the other sciences and engineering

00:05:23.924 --> 00:05:29.262
Whether you're going to study chemistry or physics, even when you look at something like accounting.

00:05:29.263 --> 00:05:32.331
You know, from my point of view, the problems in those areas

00:05:32.332 --> 00:05:36.102
are really applied math problems in engineering, or physics.

00:05:36.103 --> 00:05:40.406
And so I realized that math became somewhat of a gatekeeper

00:05:40.407 --> 00:05:47.079
to many other areas. And I took my interest in helping other people to do math

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:52.084
and my understanding of the relationship between math and other disciplines

00:05:52.085 --> 00:05:57.390
as a sign for me to think about really focusing on teaching math

00:05:57.391 --> 00:06:01.561
and then to focus on helping more students to become interested in math,

00:06:01.562 --> 00:06:06.332
science, and engineering. And what happened as I got older was this,

00:06:06.333 --> 00:06:11.270
I began to realize that many of the problems that we face in our world,

00:06:11.271 --> 00:06:16.042
not just in our country, whether they're problems involving economics

00:06:16.043 --> 00:06:21.414
and understanding the challenges we face with financial situations in America.

00:06:21.415 --> 00:06:26.552
All we're looking at are the probability we'll see certain kinds of

00:06:26.553 --> 00:06:28.821
environmental issues in different countries

00:06:28.822 --> 00:06:31.357
or we're thinking about health disparities,

00:06:31.358 --> 00:06:36.529
where often such areas as statistics and math will be very important.

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:41.100
Because the scientists and the engineers use the tools of math

00:06:41.101 --> 00:06:45.771
in solving problems. Well I'm at a university that really enjoys

00:06:45.772 --> 00:06:49.509
seeing students excel and about half of our students are in

00:06:49.510 --> 00:06:50.977
math, science, and engineering.

00:06:50.978 --> 00:06:56.549
And what is especially important to think about when you think

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:58.985
about producing students who will become scientists,

00:06:58.986 --> 00:07:04.557
is that for any student, being able to do well in math in high school

00:07:04.558 --> 00:07:09.695
and then in college, will determine in large part the ability of

00:07:09.696 --> 00:07:13.499
that student to become a physician, an engineer, or a scientist.

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:18.671
But also an accountant, as I said, or an economist, or somebody in the business world.

00:07:18.672 --> 00:07:22.408
Wherever you go, more and more, you're going to see people in mathematics

00:07:22.409 --> 00:07:26.712
or people with math backgrounds. And if you think about people in the social sciences,

00:07:26.713 --> 00:07:30.449
if you're interested in things involving sociology, or psychology.

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:34.120
Again the quantitative methods piece will be very important

00:07:34.121 --> 00:07:40.092
because we don't solve problems and draw conclusions based on anecdotal information.

00:07:40.093 --> 00:07:43.763
We really do need to be able to look at data, for example,

00:07:43.764 --> 00:07:48.234
and analyze it, and use statistical measures, and look at the average,

00:07:48.235 --> 00:07:52.138
or look at the highest frequency, or look at the probability that

00:07:52.139 --> 00:07:57.710
something will happen. And more and more, as people become these different professions,

00:07:57.711 --> 00:08:01.480
whether it is in the social sciences or the natural sciences or engineering,

00:08:01.481 --> 00:08:03.883
they see the importance of mathematics.

00:08:03.884 --> 00:08:07.787
What's been fascinating to me over the past 40 years,

00:08:07.788 --> 00:08:13.626
is to understand that even in my work as an administrator at a college,

00:08:13.627 --> 00:08:17.463
as president of the University of Maryland, Baltimore county here in D.C.

00:08:17.464 --> 00:08:21.534
Math comes in handy every day, in the work that I do.

00:08:21.535 --> 00:08:25.638
Whether it's talking about issues involving the budget, or looking at,

00:08:25.639 --> 00:08:29.742
what we call, analytics, how you go about analyzing trends

00:08:29.743 --> 00:08:31.844
and what's happening at the university.

00:08:31.845 --> 00:08:35.581
And even when I think about trying to encourage people

00:08:35.582 --> 00:08:38.518
to go into different disciplines. Let me give you an example,

00:08:38.519 --> 00:08:42.088
we work heavily with the National Security Agency.

00:08:42.089 --> 00:08:45.725
A lot of my students are interested in intelligence work and defense.

00:08:45.726 --> 00:08:51.764
And NSA said to us, we need you to help us produce more mathematicians of all kinds.

00:08:51.765 --> 00:08:55.968
Because this will be very important to have people from different perspectives,

00:08:55.969 --> 00:09:00.072
people of all races, people of all ethnicities, who know enough math

00:09:00.073 --> 00:09:02.642
so that they can be involved in helping to serve the country.

00:09:02.643 --> 00:09:05.711
There's nothing more noble, I think, than serving one's country,

00:09:05.712 --> 00:09:10.149
or saving a life, or working on the problems that can help life to be better for all of us.

00:09:10.150 --> 00:09:14.420
And what I found is that when working with NSA and talking with students,

00:09:14.421 --> 00:09:17.089
the first thing they'll say is, "Well, how will I use the math?"

00:09:17.090 --> 00:09:22.295
And when students get chances to have internships and to work in places

00:09:22.296 --> 00:09:27.500
around the country, but in the science and technology infrastructure of the country,

00:09:27.501 --> 00:09:31.704
places like the National Institutes of Health, NASA, or the National Security Agency.

00:09:31.705 --> 00:09:35.007
All of a sudden, they begin to see how the math and the science

00:09:35.008 --> 00:09:38.678
they've had in their classes will relate to the real world.

00:09:38.679 --> 00:09:43.416
And for me, explaining that foundation of math

00:09:43.417 --> 00:09:45.751
for all these areas has always been exciting.

00:09:45.752 --> 00:09:49.222
Now what I tend to do when I'm talking to students,

00:09:49.223 --> 00:09:54.560
whether they are 11 or 20, is to give them a math problem.

00:09:54.561 --> 00:09:58.865
If you're going to talk about math, why not simply give people a math problem?

00:09:58.866 --> 00:10:04.403
Here's an example of the problem I would give, and what I always tell students is,

00:10:04.404 --> 00:10:08.441
don't answer immediately, I want you to take the time to think about it.

00:10:08.442 --> 00:10:12.011
The problem says this, and I remember, as I give you this math problem,

00:10:12.012 --> 00:10:14.514
I'm getting goosebumps -- goosebumps!

00:10:14.515 --> 00:10:20.419
29 children are in a class, 20 have dogs, 15 have cats,

00:10:20.420 --> 00:10:24.156
how many have both -- a dog and a cat?

00:10:24.157 --> 00:10:27.460
I actually found that problem in The Baltimore Sun,

00:10:27.461 --> 00:10:30.496
and it was saying that many teachers and others were having problems with it.

00:10:30.497 --> 00:10:35.301
Well, for most people, even people who like math, you'll find that

00:10:35.302 --> 00:10:39.372
they'll want to give you an answer quickly. And the fact is,

00:10:39.373 --> 00:10:42.175
that most of the time, that answer is not correct.

00:10:42.176 --> 00:10:47.780
What has to happen is, from my perspective, is that the teacher

00:10:47.781 --> 00:10:50.249
or whoever is working with you on that problem has to say, "We'll draw it!"

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:54.253
And the first thing people will do if they've had algebra is to give me Venn diagrams,

00:10:54.254 --> 00:11:00.293
and I'll say, "I don't want Venn diagrams, I want you to see the children, the dogs, and the cats."

00:11:00.294 --> 00:11:05.164
Because I want people to understand that math can be related to real life,

00:11:05.165 --> 00:11:07.200
in a way that it helps explain the world.

00:11:07.201 --> 00:11:13.206
And so if you can see 3 rows, 10 kids with dogs, another 10 kids with dogs,

00:11:13.207 --> 00:11:18.444
and then put down the other 9 kids, and look at how the cats relate to that.

00:11:18.445 --> 00:11:21.480
What you'll have to see, because I'm not going to give you the answer,

00:11:21.481 --> 00:11:25.985
is that it takes strong thinking skills, it takes patience,

00:11:25.986 --> 00:11:30.590
and it takes the ability to understand how one thing connects to another.

00:11:30.591 --> 00:11:35.094
The foundation for me in math: thinking skills, reading skills,

00:11:35.095 --> 00:11:38.097
the importance of language, having the patience to look at

00:11:38.098 --> 00:11:39.432
the problem from different perspectives.

00:11:39.433 --> 00:11:46.873
I can't think of any profession that's more interesting or fascinating than mathematics.

00:11:46.874 --> 00:11:47.774
Thank you.

